#text(size: 16pt, weight: "bold", fill: report-accent)[Pilot-to-growth scaling gates]
#v(0.18em)
#text(size: 7.8pt, fill: luma(95))[Chapter 17: Scaling, Backups, and Infrastructure Migration · Implementation procedure 01 of 11]
#v(0.45em)
#grid(columns: (1fr, 1fr), gutter: 12pt,
[
*1. Current architecture and decision boundary*
#v(0.22em)
This chapter gives concrete steps for growth, backup, restore testing, and provider migration. Supabase is an integrated Auth, database, RLS, Storage, realtime, and RPC platform, so moving away requires replacing all of those responsibilities.
#v(0.35em)
*2. Source map and dependencies*
#v(0.22em)
#text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 6.85pt)[Team_Handover_2026/03_SCALING_AND_MIGRATION_RUNBOOK.md · Supabase project · Storage manifests · DNS records · target infrastructure]
#v(0.28em)
Separate source, schema, database data, Storage objects, credentials, domains, build identities, and provider configuration. A migration succeeds only after each responsibility is moved, verified, and recoverable.
#v(0.35em)
*3. Before you change anything*
#v(0.22em)
Define the desired observable result for pilot-to-growth scaling gates, identify the affected users and private data, list the routes/screens and database objects touched, and decide whether the change is reversible. Make a before-state record with source commit, current status, error text if any, and a safe test account or record. Never use a live member’s private document, payment proof, or message as a development fixture.
],
[
*4. What to do and how to do it*
#v(0.22em)
1. Define the growth threshold using real metrics: active users, messages, posts, Storage objects, query latency, support queue volume, and deployment/cold-start behavior. Do not design a million-user architecture without a measured trigger.
#v(0.22em)
2. Create separate source, schema, data, and Storage backups with timestamp, checksum, object manifest, source commit, and retention rule. Restore a representative backup into an isolated environment on a documented schedule.
#v(0.22em)
3. For Render migration, reproduce environment variables in a target secret manager, deploy the same commit to staging, test OAuth/origins, lower DNS TTL, switch only after end-to-end checks, and retain a rollback domain path.
#v(0.22em)
4. For Supabase migration, replace Postgres, Auth, RLS authorization, Storage signing, RPC behavior, realtime, and notification responsibilities one by one. Rehearse import, compare row/object counts and critical flows, then conduct a timed cutover with rollback.
])
#v(0.45em)
*5. Test, release, and rollback evidence*
#v(0.22em)
Run: #text(font: "DejaVu Sans Mono", size: 7.15pt)[Rehearse source/schema/data/Storage export and restore in isolation; compare checksums, row counts, object counts, and critical flows]. Then exercise the user journey with the correct actor and a denied actor where authorization applies. Verify the rendered result, database/RPC result, private-media access outcome, notifications if relevant, and server/device logs. Record the approved commit and release identity. If the change fails, stop further writes, return the application to the prior verified revision, preserve evidence, and correct data only through an authorized migration or protected administrative action.
#v(0.42em)
*6. Future growth and maintenance*
#v(0.22em)
At migration scale, rehearse export/import with row counts, foreign-key checks, Storage object manifests, signed-link replacement, and a tested DNS rollback before moving live traffic.
#v(0.42em)
*7. Future infrastructure change or provider migration*
#v(0.22em)
Use a staged cutover: inventory, export, target build, rehearsal restore, functional comparison, short write freeze or delta strategy, DNS/API switch, monitoring, and reversible rollback. Do not move production directly from a zip file.
#align(right)[#text(size: 7pt, fill: luma(112))[BridgeX technical handover · page 179 of 200]]
]

